WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
Breaking news.

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:04.310
The 10-day ordeal, 12 young
boys and their soccer coach

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:07.540
found alive in a flooded cave in Thailand.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.670
Narrator: Remember when that happened?

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.070
It was one of the most difficult

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.910
rescue missions of all time,

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:15.646
involving as many as 10,000 people,

00:00:15.646 --> 00:00:19.150
and now we know that
rescuers gave the boys a drug

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:21.380
which helped the mission succeed.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.963
That drug was ketamine.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.450
Ketamine was first
synthesized in the 1960s

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.880
as an anesthetic, a sedative.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.270
That's why it was so useful
in the rescue mission.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.110
But ketamine actually has
a lot of different uses.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:38.910
In the '80s, it became
a popular club drug,

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:43.310
gaining nicknames like
Special K and Cat Valium,

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.910
and more recently, in early 2019,

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:47.210
a form of ketamine was approved

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:49.600
by the FDA as an antidepressant.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.600
So how does a drug that's so
multipurpose actually work?

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.430
Ketamine is what scientists
call a dirty drug.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.610
That means it doesn't just target

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:03.670
one system in your brain, but dozens.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:05.570
It has a weak effect on opiate receptors

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:07.010
in the dopamine system,

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:09.740
which drugs like heroine
and cocaine target.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.260
But most importantly, ketamine manipulates

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.940
a neurotransmitter called glutamate,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.090
earning it the attention of
psychiatrists nationwide.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.250
Glutamate is what many of
the neurons in your brain

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.310
use to communicate with each other,

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:26.350
and without it, well, your brain
would essentially shut down

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.670
like a city grid without power.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:32.429
Now, at high doses, ketamine
seems to block glutamate.

00:01:32.429 --> 00:01:35.990
That's why it's such an
effective anesthetic.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:39.210
But in low doses, like what
you might find at a club

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:42.460
or in a spray of the
FDA-approved drug esketamine,

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.950
it actually ramps up glutamate production,

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.000
and that comes with all
kinds of side effects.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.960
It can make you hallucinate
or feel as though

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:52.600
you're losing touch with reality.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.090
And it might also help build
new connections or synapses

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:00.280
between neurons, electrifying
new parts of that city grid.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:03.070
Chadi Abdallah: When people
are stressed for a long time

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:06.350
or when they suffer from
depression for a while,

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.390
they start losing these connections,

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:11.174
and when we give them ketamine 24 hours,

00:02:11.174 --> 00:02:14.879
they reverse. Now they look
like more a normal brain.

00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:18.440
So we believe that perhaps ketamine

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.040
is working by regenerating
these connections

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.650
that are needed for
normal brain functioning.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:25.970
Narrator: And that could make ketamine

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.310
one of the best drugs out
there for treating depression.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.810
This isn't how other antidepressants work.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.120
Medications like Prozac and
Zoloft take weeks if not months

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.750
to kick in, and they regulate serotonin,

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:38.960
another chemical in the brain,

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.340
which scientists have
long tied to depression.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.630
These more traditional drugs
may work for some people,

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.140
but not for everyone.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.310
In fact, as many as 4
million American adults

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.300
have treatment-resistant depression.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.983
And for them, well, ketamine might be

00:02:53.983 --> 00:02:56.591
the only drug out there
that can provide relief.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.220
That's according to Doctor Andre Atoian.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.400
He's the founder of Ketamine Specialists,

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.500
a clinic where he administers
ketamine to patients with

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.453
mood disorders, pain, and addiction.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.560
Andre Atoian: Ketamine is
the agent that works when

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.280
most others have failed.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.890
It is something that really
allows us to give patients

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:19.750
a sort of new hope 'cause a
lot of people that I treat

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.380
have basically already tried everything,

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.590
and they're in this situation

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:26.410
where nothing's really working,

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.800
and they're suffering, they're miserable.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.170
Narrator: So it's hard to
deny that ketamine's effect

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.260
on depression sounds promising,

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.610
and, clearly, it's a useful sedative.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.080
But here's the thing...

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.864
Nolan Williams: As far as
effect, it's, you know,

00:03:38.864 --> 00:03:40.930
there's still a lot we don't know.

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:42.540
Narrator: That's Doctor Nolan Williams,

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.190
an assistant professor of psychiatry

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.430
at the Stanford University Medical Center.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:49.030
He says that because ketamine
manipulates so many different

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:50.570
receptors in the brain,

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:52.750
it's been hard to nail down all of them.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.360
So it's still unclear how it
might affect different patients

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.130
both short- and long-term,

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:58.990
and researchers still haven't figured out

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:01.370
how to preserve its benefits
as an antidepressant.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:03.228
Abdallah: The challenge is how to maintain

00:04:03.228 --> 00:04:05.019
these new connections.

00:04:05.019 --> 00:04:07.340
Is it to give ketamine repeatedly?

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:10.240
We know that we cannot give it every day,

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:11.910
that's like substance of abuse,

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.280
because then it will harm the brain.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.810
Narrator: So while FDA
approval is considered

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:18.550
a victory for the millions of people

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:20.360
with treatment-resistant depression,

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.825
doctors still caution that
ketamine should only be used

00:04:22.825 --> 00:04:24.253
as a last resort.

